<article class="env-proposition" id="00CS">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00CS">Proposition <span data-tag="00CS">10.9.12</span>.</a> Let $L\xrightarrow {u} M\xrightarrow {v} N$ be an exact sequence of $R$-modules. Then $S^{-1}L \to S^{-1}M \to S^{-1}N$ is also exact. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      First it is clear that $S^{-1}L \to S^{-1}M \to S^{-1}N$ is a complex since localization is a functor. Next suppose that $x/s$ maps to zero in $S^{-1}N$ for some $x/s \in S^{-1}M$. Then by definition there is a $t\in S$ such that $v(xt) = v(x)t = 0$ in $N$, which means $xt \in \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(v)$. By the exactness of $L \to M \to N$ we have $xt = u(y)$ for some $y$ in $L$. Then $x/s$ is the image of $y/st$. This proves the exactness. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>